# c2: --strict
seg-e0f2ddce6dff = <c1/> runs the implementation against the document's printed tables and worked examples (bars B1 to B10 of <a1>the pre-registration</a1>) and prints each with its count and status. <c2/> exits { "1" } unless every bar is met.

# text
# c1: symbols
# c2: 0
# c3: 1
seg-dafa47406a38 = <c1/> reads a hard-decision symbol stream (text of <c2/> and <c3/>; white space ignored) and checks the synchronisation pattern, SB1 decoding, FID (only { "0" } is defined), TOI continuity (advancing by one modulo { "100" }) and frame spacing ({ "6000" } symbols). It exits { "1" } on any problem.

# text
# c1: generate
seg-f12425044025 = <c1/> writes a test stream: the document's synchronisation pattern, SB1 and frame lengths, with <b>placeholder</b> subframes { "2" } to { "4" } (the alternating fill of LSIS-300, not LDPC coded, no message). It is symbols for testing a checker or a decoder, not a waveform; Kshana writes no signal.

